// Private relay networks: relays that carry one tenant's traffic and nobody
// else's.
//
// A pool is a set of relays that belongs to one tenant. Making the tenant
// private confines it to the pool, and the confinement is not configuration
// alone: every token minted for a private tenant names the pool in a sealed
// `pool` claim, a pool relay refuses every token that does not name its pool,
// and a shared relay refuses every token that names one. So an app pointed at
// the wrong relay fails closed rather than falling back, and there is no path
// from a private tenant onto the shared fleet -- not when the pool is full, and
// not when it has no relays yet.
//
// Owner-only. A pool decides where every byte of the tenant's media goes and
// what the tenant is billed for, which is not something a key issued to mint
// tokens or read dashboards should be able to change.

